var e=JSON.parse(`[{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":1,"q":"In the Government of India Act 1919, the functions of Provincial Government were divided into 'Reserved' and 'Transferred' subjects. Which of the following were treated as 'Reserved' subjects?\\n1. Administration of Justice\\n2. Local Self-Government\\n3. Land Revenue\\n4. Police\\n\\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["1, 2 and 3","2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1, 3 and 4 were Reserved subjects:</b> Under the Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms (Government of India Act, 1919), Dyarchy was introduced in the provinces. 'Reserved' subjects (administered by the Governor and his Executive Council without legislative responsibility) included Police, Administration of Justice, Land Revenue, Irrigation, and Finance.</p><p><b>Local Self-Government</b>, Education, Public Health, and Agriculture were 'Transferred' subjects administered by Ministers responsible to the provincial legislature.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":2,"q":"In medieval India, the term 'Fanam' referred to:","options":["Clothing","Coins","Ornaments","Weapons"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> In medieval South India (including the Vijayanagar Empire and Travancore/Malabar kingdoms), <b>Fanam</b> (or <i>Panam</i>) was a standard denomination of small gold or silver <b>coins</b> used for everyday commercial transactions.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":3,"q":"Consider the following freedom fighters:\\n1. Barindra Kumar Ghosh\\n2. Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee\\n3. Rash Behari Bose\\n\\nWho of the above was/were actively associated with the Ghadar Party?","options":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 4:</b> <b>Rash Behari Bose</b> was the master coordinator who linked up with the international Ghadar Party network to organize the planned pan-India armed revolt of February 1915 among British Indian army garrisons, before escaping to Japan.</p><p>Barindra Kumar Ghosh was associated with the Jugantar/Anushilan Samiti in Bengal, and Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee was a founding leader of the Hindustan Republican Association (HRA).</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":4,"q":"With reference to the proposals of Cripps Mission, consider the following statements:\\n1. The Constituent Assembly would have members nominated by the Provincial Assemblies as well as the Princely States.\\n2. Any Province, which is not prepared to accept the new Constitution would have the right to sign a separate agreement with Britain regarding its future status.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement 2 is correct:</b> The Cripps proposals (1942) contained a non-accession clause permitting any province that did not wish to accept the new Constitution to retain its constitutional status or frame a separate constitution with the British Crown.</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> The Constituent Assembly members from British provinces were to be <i>elected</i> through proportional representation by the lower houses of the Provincial Assemblies, while only princely states would <i>nominate</i> their representatives.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":5,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following texts:\\n1. Nettipakarana\\n2. Parishishtaparvan\\n3. Avadanashataka\\n4. Trishashtilakshana Mahapurana\\n\\nWhich of the above are Jaina texts?","options":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Texts 2 and 4 are Jaina texts:</b></p><ul><li><b>Parishishtaparvan:</b> 12th-century Sanskrit poetic treatise on Jaina history composed by the celebrated Jain polymath Hemachandra.</li><li><b>Trishashtilakshana Mahapurana:</b> 9th-century Sanskrit Digambara Jaina epic started by Jinasena and completed by Gunabhadra.</li><li><i>Nettipakarana</i> and <i>Avadanashataka</i> are canonical and non-canonical Buddhist texts.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":6,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following pairs:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Historical Personality</th><th>Known as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Aryadeva</td><td>Jaina scholar</td></tr><tr><td>2. Dignaga</td><td>Buddhist scholar</td></tr><tr><td>3. Nathamuni</td><td>Vaishnava scholar</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["None of the pairs","Only one pair","Only two pairs","All three pairs"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only two pairs (2 and 3) are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Dignaga:</b> Famous 5th-century Buddhist logician and founder of the Buddhist epistemological school (Pramana). (Correct)</li><li><b>Nathamuni:</b> 9th–10th century Vaishnavite acharya who collected and canonized the 4,000 Tamil hymns of the Alvars (Divya Prabandham). (Correct)</li><li>Pair 1 is incorrect: <b>Aryadeva</b> was a renowned 3rd-century Mahayana <b>Buddhist scholar</b> and foremost disciple of Nagarjuna in the Madhyamaka school, not a Jaina scholar.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":7,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following statements:\\n1. The first Mongol invasion of India happened during the reign of Jalal-ud-din Khalji.\\n2. During the reign of Ala-ud-din Khalji, one Mongol assault marched up to Delhi and besieged the city.\\n3. Muhammad-bin-Tughlaq temporarily lost portions of north-west of his kingdom to Mongols.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement 2 is correct:</b> In 1299 and 1303, massive Mongol armies under Qutlugh Khwaja and Targhi invaded the Sultanate, directly surrounded Delhi, and besieged the fortress of Siri during Alauddin Khalji's reign.</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> The first major Mongol threat occurred much earlier in 1221 during the reign of <b>Shamsuddin Iltutmish</b> when Genghis Khan pursued Jalal-ud-din Mangabarni to the banks of the Indus River.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Muhammad bin Tughlaq decisively defeated the Mongol invasion led by Tarmashirin and maintained firm control over northwestern frontiers.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":8,"q":"With reference to Indian history, who of the following were known as 'Kulah-Daran'?","options":["Qalandars","Persian calligraphists","Ulama","Sayyids"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 4:</b> The <b>Sayyids</b> (who traced their lineage directly from the Prophet Muhammad) were distinguished by wearing a tall pointed cap/headgear called the <i>Kulah</i>, earning them the historical title of <b>Kulah-Daran</b> in Sultanate courts.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":9,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following statements:\\n1. The Dutch established their factories/warehouses on the east coast on lands granted to them by Gajapati rulers.\\n2. Alfonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate.\\n3. The English East India Company established a factory at Madras on a plot of land leased from a representative of the Vijayanagara Empire.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are correct:</b> Alfonso de Albuquerque captured Goa in 1510 from Yusuf Adil Shah of Bijapur. Francis Day of the English East India Company secured the land lease for Fort St. George at Madras in 1639 from Damarla Venkatadri Nayaka, a feudatory of Chandragiri (the remnants of the Vijayanagar Empire).</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> The Gajapati dynasty of Odisha collapsed in the mid-16th century (1541), long before the Dutch arrived in India and obtained trading rights in the 17th century from the Golconda Sultanate.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":10,"q":"According to Kautilya's Arthashastra, which of the following are correct?\\n1. A person could be a slave as a result of a judicial punishment.\\n2. If a female slave bore her master a son, she was legally free.\\n3. If a son born to a female slave was fathered by her master, the son was entitled to the legal status of the master's son.\\n\\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> In the <i>Arthashastra</i>, Kautilya details slavery (Dasa) jurisprudence: criminal penalties could lead to enslavement (Dandapranita), a female slave fathered by her master earned automatic emancipation along with her child, and the male child was legally recognized with full inheritance rights as a legitimate son of the master.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":11,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\n1. Tight monetary policy of US Federal Reserve could lead to capital flight.\\n2. Capital flight may increase the interest cost of firms with existing External Commercial Borrowings (ECBs).\\n3. Devaluation of domestic currency decreases the currency risk associated with ECBs.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Rate hikes by the US Fed trigger capital outflows from emerging markets toward dollar assets (capital flight), which puts depreciation pressure on local currencies and increases borrowing and refinancing costs on existing foreign debt.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Devaluation/depreciation of the domestic currency increases the servicing cost in rupee terms for unhedged foreign debt, thereby <i>increasing</i> (not decreasing) the currency risk of ECBs.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":12,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding Credit Rating Agencies (CRAs) in India:\\n1. In India, Credit Rating Agencies are regulated by the Reserve Bank of India.\\n2. The rating agency popularly known as ICRA is a public limited company.\\n3. Brickwork Ratings is an Indian credit rating agency.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are correct:</b> ICRA is a publicly listed corporate credit rating agency, and Brickwork Ratings is a registered Indian credit rating agency.</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> Credit Rating Agencies in India are registered and regulated by the <b>Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI)</b> under the SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999, not the RBI.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":13,"q":"With reference to the 'Banks Board Bureau' (BBB), consider the following statements:\\n1. The Governor of RBI is the Chairman of BBB.\\n2. BBB recommends for the selection of heads for Public Sector Banks.\\n3. BBB helps the Public Sector Banks in developing strategies and capital raising plans.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are correct:</b> The Banks Board Bureau (now restructured as the Financial Services Institutions Bureau - FSIB) was constituted based on the P.J. Nayak Committee to recommend whole-time directors and chiefs for Public Sector Banks and advise banks on capital planning and governance strategies.</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> The Chairman of the BBB is an eminent person/former civil servant nominated by the Central Government, not the Governor of the RBI.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":14,"q":"With reference to 'Convertible Bonds', consider the following statements:\\n1. As there is an option to exchange the bond for equity, convertible bonds pay a lower rate of interest.\\n2. The option to convert to equity affords the bondholder a degree of indexation to rising consumer prices.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b></p><ul><li><b>Statement 1:</b> Because investors receive the upside option to convert the debt into company stock, issuers can offer lower coupon interest rates compared to straight non-convertible debt.</li><li><b>Statement 2:</b> Equity shares generally appreciate with economic expansion and price inflation, providing convertible bondholders with an intrinsic inflation-hedge mechanism when converting to equity.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":15,"q":"Consider the following:\\n1. Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank\\n2. Missile Technology Control Regime\\n3. Shanghai Cooperation Organisation\\n\\nIndia is a member of which of the above?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three:</b> India is a founding member and second-largest shareholder of the <b>Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB)</b>; joined the <b>Missile Technology Control Regime (MTCR)</b> in 2016 as its 35th member; and became a full permanent member of the <b>Shanghai Cooperation Organisation (SCO)</b> in 2017.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":16,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding Vietnam:\\n1. Vietnam has been one of the fastest growing economies in the world in the recent past.\\n2. Vietnam is led by a multi-party political system.\\n3. Vietnam's economic growth is linked to its integration with global supply chains and focus on exports.\\n4. For a long time low labour costs and stable exchange rates have attracted foreign investment to Vietnam.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2","2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1, 3 and 4 are correct:</b> Vietnam's manufacturing-led export boom is driven by open trade integration (EVFTA, CPTPP), competitive labor costs, and currency stability.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> Vietnam is a <b>one-party socialist republic</b> governed exclusively by the Communist Party of Vietnam (CPV), with no multi-party political system.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":17,"q":"In India, which one of the following is responsible for maintaining price stability by controlling inflation?","options":["Department of Consumer Affairs","Expenditure Management Commission","Financial Stability and Development Council","Reserve Bank of India"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 4:</b> Under the amended Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934 (via the 2016 monetary policy framework amendment), the <b>Reserve Bank of India (RBI)</b> through the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) is statutorily mandated with the primary objective of maintaining <b>price stability</b> while keeping in mind the objective of growth (targeting 4% +/- 2% CPI inflation).</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":18,"q":"With reference to Non-Fungible Tokens (NFTs), consider the following statements:\\n1. They enable the digital representation of physical assets.\\n2. They are unique cryptographic tokens that exist on a blockchain.\\n3. They can be traded or exchanged at equivalency and therefore can be used as a medium of commercial transactions.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> NFTs are unique, indivisible cryptographic assets on a distributed blockchain ledger representing ownership of digital or real-world physical items (art, music, land titles).</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Because they are non-fungible (each token is distinct and unique), they cannot be exchanged interchangeably at mutual equivalency like fiat currency or Bitcoin, and thus do not function as a standard fungible medium of exchange.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":19,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Reservoir</th><th>State</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Ghataprabha</td><td>Telangana</td></tr><tr><td>2. Gandhi Sagar</td><td>Madhya Pradesh</td></tr><tr><td>3. Indira Sagar</td><td>Andhra Pradesh</td></tr><tr><td>4. Maithon</td><td>Jharkhand</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nHow many pairs given above are not correctly matched?","options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only two pairs (1 and 3) are not correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Ghataprabha Reservoir:</b> Located in Belagavi district, <b>Karnataka</b> (not Telangana).</li><li><b>Indira Sagar Dam:</b> Located on the Narmada River in Khandwa district, <b>Madhya Pradesh</b> (not Andhra Pradesh).</li><li>Gandhi Sagar (Chambal River, MP) and Maithon (Barakar River, Jharkhand) are correctly matched.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":20,"q":"In India, which one of the following compiles information on industrial disputes, closures, retrenchments and lay-offs in factories employing workers?","options":["Central Statistics Office","Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade","Labour Bureau","National Technical Manpower Information System"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 3:</b> The <b>Labour Bureau</b>, an attached office under the Ministry of Labour and Employment, is responsible for the collection, compilation, and dissemination of statistics on industrial disputes, closures, retrenchments, absenteeism, and lay-offs in India.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":21,"q":"With reference to the 'Tea Board' in India, consider the following statements:\\n1. The Tea Board is a statutory body.\\n2. It is a regulatory body attached to the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare.\\n3. The Tea Board's Head Office is situated in Bengaluru.\\n4. The Board has overseas offices at Dubai and Moscow.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 3","2 and 4","3 and 4","1 and 4"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 4 are correct:</b> The Tea Board of India is a statutory body established under Section 4 of the Tea Act, 1953, and maintains foreign promotional offices in Dubai and Moscow.</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are incorrect:</b> The Tea Board functions under the <b>Ministry of Commerce and Industry</b> (not Agriculture), and its Head Office is located in <b>Kolkata, West Bengal</b> (not Bengaluru).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":22,"q":"Which one of the following best describes the term 'Greenwashing'?","options":["Conveying a false impression that a company's products are more environmentally sound and eco-friendly than they actually are","Non-inclusion of ecological costs in the Annual Financial Statements of a country","Ignoring the disastrous ecological consequences while undertaking developmental activities","Making mandatory provisions for environmental costs in government project tenders"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> <b>Greenwashing</b> is a deceptive marketing practice where a corporation exaggerates, misleads, or fabricates claims regarding the environmental friendliness, sustainability, or carbon-neutrality of its products or corporate practices to project an eco-conscious image.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":23,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\n1. High clouds primarily reflect solar radiation and cool the surface of the Earth.\\n2. Low clouds have a high absorption of infrared radiation that emanates from the Earth's surface and thus cause warming effect.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements are incorrect (cross-matched):</b></p><ul><li><b>High thin clouds (such as cirrus):</b> Are relatively transparent to incoming shortwave solar radiation but trap outgoing longwave terrestrial infrared radiation, causing a net <i>warming effect</i>.</li><li><b>Low thick clouds (such as stratocumulus):</b> Have high optical thickness and high albedo, reflecting large amounts of incoming solar radiation back into space, causing a net <i>cooling effect</i>.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":24,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'System of Rice Intensification' (SRI) of cultivation, in which alternate wetting and drying is practiced:\\n1. Reduced seed requirement\\n2. Reduced methane production\\n3. Reduced electricity consumption\\n\\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> System of Rice Intensification (SRI) uses single young seedlings planted at wider spacing (reducing seed requirement by ~80–90%), avoids continuous flooding by practicing intermittent wetting and drying (aerobic soil conditions suppress anaerobic methanogenesis, curbing methane emissions), and drastically reduces pump irrigation and electricity use.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":25,"q":"Which one of the following lakes of West Africa has become dry and turned into a desert due to severe climatic drying and water diversion?","options":["Lake Victoria","Lake Faguibine","Lake Oguta","Lake Volta"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> <b>Lake Faguibine</b> in northern Mali (West Africa), once part of an expansive fertile inland delta system fed by annual flooding of the Niger River, dried up completely in the late 20th century following prolonged Sahelian droughts and sand dune blockages.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":26,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Major Non-NATO Ally' (MNNA) status:\\n1. It is a designation given by the United States government to close allies that have strategic working relationships with the US Armed Forces but are not members of NATO.\\n2. The designation automatically confers a mutual defence pact with the United States.\\n3. India was granted Major Non-NATO Ally status in 2019.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement 1 is correct:</b> Major Non-NATO Ally (MNNA) is a statutory designation under US law providing foreign partners with specific military and financial advantages in defence trade and security cooperation without establishing a mutual defence pact.</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are incorrect:</b> MNNA status does not entail automatic mutual defence commitments (unlike Article 5 of NATO). India is designated as a 'Major Defence Partner' (MDP) by the US, not a Major Non-NATO Ally (a status held by countries like Japan, South Korea, Israel, and Pakistan).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":27,"q":"Consider the following pairs of peaks and the mountain ranges in which they are situated:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Peak</th><th>Mountain Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Namcha Barwa</td><td>Garhwal Himalaya</td></tr><tr><td>2. Nanda Devi</td><td>Kumaon Himalaya</td></tr><tr><td>3. Nokrek</td><td>Sikkim Himalaya</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Pair 2 is correctly matched:</b> Nanda Devi (7,816 m) is located in the Garhwal/Kumaon Himalayan section in Uttarakhand.</p><p><b>Pairs 1 and 3 are incorrect:</b> Namcha Barwa (7,782 m) is situated in the Eastern Himalayas / Assam Himalaya (Tibet/Arunachal border near the Great Bend of the Tsangpo/Brahmaputra). Nokrek is the highest peak of the Garo Hills in Meghalaya, not the Sikkim Himalayas.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":28,"q":"The term 'Levant' often heard in the news roughly corresponds to which of the following geographical regions?","options":["Region along the eastern Mediterranean shores","Region along North African shores stretching from Egypt to Morocco","Region along Persian Gulf and Horn of Africa","The entire coastal area of Mediterranean Sea"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> The <b>Levant</b> is a historical and geographical term referring to the large area along the <b>eastern Mediterranean shores</b>, encompassing modern-day Syria, Lebanon, Israel, Palestine, Jordan, and parts of southern Turkey.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":29,"q":"Consider the following countries:\\n1. Azerbaijan\\n2. Kyrgyzstan\\n3. Tajikistan\\n4. Turkmenistan\\n5. Uzbekistan\\n\\nWhich of the above have borders with Afghanistan?","options":["1, 2 and 5","1, 2, 3 and 4","3, 4 and 5","2, 3 and 5"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Countries 3, 4 and 5 share borders with Afghanistan:</b> Afghanistan is bordered by six countries: Tajikistan, Turkmenistan, and Uzbekistan to the north; Iran to the west; Pakistan to the east and south; and China via the narrow Wakhan Corridor to the northeast.</p><p>Azerbaijan (Caucasus) and Kyrgyzstan do not share a land boundary with Afghanistan.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":30,"q":"With reference to India, consider the following statements:\\n1. Monazite is a source of rare earths.\\n2. Monazite contains thorium.\\n3. Monazite occurs naturally in the entire Indian coastal sands in India.\\n4. In India, Government-designated bodies alone can process or export monazite.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 4 only","3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct:</b> Monazite is a phosphate mineral containing rare earth elements (cerium, lanthanum, neodymium) and significant amounts of radioactive thorium. Because thorium is a prescribed strategic substance under the Atomic Energy Act, 1962, handling, processing, and exporting monazite is strictly restricted to government-designated entities (such as Indian Rare Earths Limited - IREL).</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Monazite does not occur along the 'entire' coastline; it occurs in rich placer concentrations in specific coastal stretches (notably Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Odisha, and Andhra Pradesh).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":31,"q":"In the northern hemisphere, the longest day of the year normally occurs in the:","options":["First half of the month of June","Second half of the month of June","First half of the month of July","Second half of the month of July"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> The Summer Solstice in the Northern Hemisphere occurs annually on <b>June 20, 21, or 22</b> (which falls in the <b>second half of the month of June</b>), when the Sun reaches its highest point in the sky and the Tropic of Cancer receives direct vertical rays of the sun, marking the longest day and shortest night of the year.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":32,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Wetland / Lake</th><th>Location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Hokera Wetland</td><td>Punjab</td></tr><tr><td>2. Renuka Wetland</td><td>Himachal Pradesh</td></tr><tr><td>3. Rudrasagar Lake</td><td>Tripura</td></tr><tr><td>4. Sasthamkotta Lake</td><td>Tamil Nadu</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only two pairs (2 and 3) are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Renuka Wetland:</b> Located in Sirmaur district, Himachal Pradesh (India's smallest Ramsar site). (Correct)</li><li><b>Rudrasagar Lake:</b> Located in Sepahijala, Tripura. (Correct)</li><li>Pair 1 is incorrect: <b>Hokera (Hokersar) Wetland</b> is in <b>Jammu & Kashmir</b> (near Srinagar), not Punjab.</li><li>Pair 4 is incorrect: <b>Sasthamkotta Lake</b> (freshwater lake) is in Kollam district, <b>Kerala</b>, not Tamil Nadu.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":33,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Miyawaki method' frequently mentioned in news:\\n1. It is a Japanese method of urban afforestation designed to create dense, native miniature forests in small land parcels.\\n2. The method uses only genetically modified fast-growing exotic timber clones.\\n3. Forests grown via this method grow up to 10 times faster and are significantly denser than conventional plantations.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> Pioneered by Japanese botanist Akira Miyawaki, the technique creates multi-layered, self-sustaining native mini-forests in urban spaces that grow ~10 times faster and ~30 times denser than traditional monoculture plantations.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> The method explicitly uses only multi-tiered <b>indigenous native species</b> adapted to local ecological conditions, strictly avoiding exotic or genetically modified monocultures.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":34,"q":"With reference to the 'World Health Organization (WHO) Air Quality Guidelines', consider the following statements:\\n1. The 24-hour mean of $PM_{2.5}$ should not exceed $15\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$ and annual mean of $PM_{2.5}$ should not exceed $5\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$.\\n2. In a year, the highest levels of ozone pollution occur during the periods of inclement weather and heavy cloud cover.\\n3. $PM_{10}$ can penetrate the lung barrier and enter the blood stream.\\n4. Excessive ozone in the air can trigger asthma.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4","1 and 2 only"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 4 are correct:</b> The revised WHO Global Air Quality Guidelines (2021) recommend annual $PM_{2.5}$ limits of $5\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$ and 24-hour limits of $15\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$. Ground-level ozone $(O_3)$ is a strong respiratory irritant that aggravates asthma.</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are incorrect:</b> Ground-level ozone is a secondary photochemical pollutant requiring intense solar ultraviolet radiation and high temperatures to form (highest on hot, sunny days, not during cloud cover/inclement weather). It is fine particulate matter ($PM_{2.5}$ and ultrafine $PM_{0.1}$), rather than coarse $PM_{10}$, that easily penetrates the alveolar-capillary lung barrier directly into the bloodstream.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":35,"q":"With reference to 'Gucchi', sometimes mentioned in the news, consider the following statements:\\n1. It is a wild edible mushroom belonging to the genus Morchella.\\n2. It grows in the temperate forest soils of the Himalayan region.\\n3. It is commercially cultivated in large-scale polyhouses in the plains of north-eastern India.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Gucchi (morel mushroom / <i>Morchella esculenta</i>) is an expensive wild edible fungus that grows naturally in conifer-rich temperate forests of the Himalayas (Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand).</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Gucchi cannot be easily cultivated commercially on a farm/polyhouse scale; it is collected in the wild by local forest dwellers and villagers from forest floors.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":36,"q":"With reference to 'Polyethylene Terephthalate' (PET), the use of which is so widespread in our daily life, consider the following statements:\\n1. Its fibres can be blended with wool and cotton fibres to reinforce their properties.\\n2. Containers made of it can be used to store any alcoholic beverage without leaching.\\n3. Bottles made of it can be recycled into other products.\\n4. Articles made of it can be easily disposed of by incineration without causing greenhouse gas emissions.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only","1, 2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4 only"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> PET polyester fibres are widely blended with cotton (polycot) and wool to improve wrinkle resistance and tensile strength. PET bottles are thermoplastic and widely recycled into fleece, carpets, and new containers.</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 4 are incorrect:</b> High-proof alcoholic beverages are not stored indefinitely in standard PET containers due to alcohol acting as an organic solvent that can leach additives like acetaldehyde/antimony. Incinerating PET releases toxic gases and carbon dioxide ($CO_2$), contributing to greenhouse gas emissions.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":37,"q":"Consider the following birds:\\n1. Ceylon frogmouth\\n2. Coppersmith barbet\\n3. Gray-chinned minivet\\n4. White-throated redstart\\n\\nWhich of the above are naturally found in India?","options":["1, 2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All four birds are found in India:</b></p><ul><li><b>Ceylon frogmouth:</b> Found in the dense rainforests of the Western Ghats (and Sri Lanka).</li><li><b>Coppersmith barbet:</b> Resident resident bird across the Indian subcontinent.</li><li><b>Gray-chinned minivet:</b> Inhabits montane forests of the Himalayas and Northeast India.</li><li><b>White-throated redstart:</b> Breeds in the high-altitude Himalayan ranges of India.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":38,"q":"Which of the following are nitrogen-fixing plants?\\n1. Alfalfa\\n2. Amaranth\\n3. Chickpea\\n4. Clover\\n5. Purslane (Kulfa)\\n6. Spinach\\n\\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["1, 3 and 4 only","1, 3, 5 and 6 only","2, 4, 5 and 6 only","1, 2, 4 and 5 only"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Alfalfa (1), Chickpea (3), and Clover (4) are nitrogen-fixing plants:</b> These belong to the Fabaceae (legume) family, possessing root nodules harboring symbiotic <i>Rhizobium</i> bacteria that fix atmospheric nitrogen into nitrates.</p><p>Amaranth (Amaranthaceae), Purslane (Portulacaceae), and Spinach (Amaranthaceae) are non-leguminous green leafy herbs that do not fix atmospheric nitrogen.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":39,"q":"'Biorock technology' is talked about in which of the following situations?","options":["Restoration of damaged coral reefs","Development of building materials using plant residues","Identification of areas for exploration/extraction of shale gas","Providing salt licks for wild animals in protected areas"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> <b>Biorock technology</b> (mineral accretion technology) involves passing low-voltage direct electrical currents through submerged steel structures. This precipitates dissolved calcium carbonate onto the frame, forming limestone coatings that accelerate the growth and calcification of transplanted <b>damaged coral reefs</b> and increase their resilience to thermal bleaching.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":40,"q":"The 'Climate Action Tracker' which monitors the emission reduction pledges of different countries is a:","options":["Database created by coalition of research organisations","Wing of the International Panel on Climate Change","Committee under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change","Agency promoted and funded by the United Nations Environment Programme and World Bank"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> The <b>Climate Action Tracker (CAT)</b> is an independent scientific project established by a consortium of climate research organizations (Climate Analytics and NewClimate Institute) tracking government climate action, NDCs, and policies against the globally agreed Paris Agreement temperature targets.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":41,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\n1. 'The Climate Group' is an international non-profit organization that drives climate action by building large networks and runs them.\\n2. The International Energy Agency in partnership with the Climate Group launched a global initiative 'EP100'.\\n3. EP100 brings together leading companies committed to driving energy productivity.\\n4. Some Indian companies are members of EP100.\\n5. The International Renewable Energy Agency (IRENA) is the secretariat to the 'Under2 Coalition'.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1, 2, 4 and 5","1, 3 and 4 only","2, 3 and 5 only","1, 2, 3, 4 and 5"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1, 3 and 4 are correct:</b> The Climate Group runs corporate climate campaigns. EP100 brings together businesses committed to doubling energy productivity (incorporating major Indian firms like Mahindra & Mahindra, Dalmia Cement, and UltraTech).</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 5 are incorrect:</b> EP100 is led by The Climate Group in partnership with the <b>Alliance to Save Energy</b> (not the IEA). The Climate Group (not IRENA) serves as the official secretariat to the Under2 Coalition.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":42,"q":"If Rainforests and Tropical Forests are the 'Lungs of the Earth', then Wetlands may be described as its:","options":["Heart","Brain","Kidneys","Liver"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 3:</b> Wetlands are commonly referred to as the <b>'Kidneys of the Earth'</b> because they filter sediments, decompose waste, trap toxic pollutants and heavy metals, and cycle nutrients, thereby purifying terrestrial runoff before it reaches larger river and marine bodies.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":43,"q":"In the context of WHO Air Quality Guidelines, consider the following statements:\\n1. The 24-hour mean of $PM_{2.5}$ should not exceed $15\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$.\\n2. The 24-hour mean of $PM_{10}$ should not exceed $45\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$.\\n3. The annual mean of $NO_2$ should not exceed $10\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct as per 2021 guidelines?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> The WHO Global Air Quality Guidelines (2021 updated thresholds) recommend:</p><ul><li>$PM_{2.5}$: 24-hour mean of $15\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$ (annual mean $5\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$).</li><li>$PM_{10}$: 24-hour mean of $45\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$ (annual mean $15\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$).</li><li>$NO_2$: Annual mean limit reduced to $10\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$ (24-hour mean $25\\\\;\\\\mu\\\\text{g/m}^3$).</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":44,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\n1. Other than human beings, no animal is known to cultivate fungi.\\n2. Some species of ants farm fungus in subterranean nests for food.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement 2 is correct:</b> Leafcutter ants (such as <i>Atta</i> and <i>Acromyrmex</i>) and certain ambrosia beetles and termites actively practice fungiculture, cutting leaves to cultivate specialized fungi in subterranean garden chambers as their primary food source.</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> Because these insect species engage in agriculture/fungus-farming, humans are not the only animals that cultivate fungi.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":45,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Indian Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972':\\n1. Dedicated protected areas like National Parks and Wildlife Sanctuaries are constituted under this Act.\\n2. Hunting of wild animals is prohibited across India except in accordance with permits granted by the Chief Wildlife Warden under specified conditions.\\n3. The Act does not provide any schedule or protection for plant species.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972 provides the legal framework for establishing National Parks, Sanctuaries, and Conservation Reserves, and Section 9 strictly prohibits hunting of specified wildlife except when authorized by the Chief Wildlife Warden for special purposes (e.g., threat to human life or diseased specimens).</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Chapter IIIA and Schedule VI (in the pre-amended Act) explicitly regulate and protect specified plant species (such as Beddome's cycad, Blue Vanda, Ladies Slipper Orchid, Pitcher plant).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":46,"q":"Consider the following pairs of international environmental initiatives and the organizations that launched them:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Initiative</th><th>Organization / Framework</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Global Environment Facility</td><td>World Bank, UNDP and UNEP</td></tr><tr><td>2. Green Climate Fund</td><td>UN Framework Convention on Climate Change</td></tr><tr><td>3. Adaptation Fund</td><td>Kyoto Protocol / Paris Agreement</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>GEF:</b> Established on the eve of the 1992 Rio Earth Summit by the World Bank in partnership with UNDP and UNEP.</li><li><b>GCF:</b> Set up by 194 countries party to the UNFCCC at COP16 in Cancun in 2010.</li><li><b>Adaptation Fund:</b> Established under the Kyoto Protocol and now serves the Paris Agreement to finance concrete adaptation projects in vulnerable developing countries.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":47,"q":"With reference to the 'System of Environmental-Economic Accounting' (SEEA) developed by the United Nations, consider the following statements:\\n1. It integrates economic and environmental data to provide a more comprehensive view of the interrelationships between the economy and the environment.\\n2. It enables the measurement of the depletion of natural resources and degradation of ecosystems in physical and monetary terms.\\n3. The Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI) has released environmental accounts for India based on SEEA.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> The SEEA is the UN standard framework for natural capital accounting. MoSPI in India compiles the 'EnviStats India' report annually based on the SEEA framework to track physical natural resource stocks and ecosystem services.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":48,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Benthos' in marine ecology:\\n1. They are organisms that live attached to or near the bottom sediments of aquatic bodies.\\n2. Corals, sea anemones, sponges, and starfish are examples of benthic marine fauna.\\n3. Benthic organisms are found exclusively in shallow coastal waters where sunlight penetrates to the bottom.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Benthic organisms (Benthos) inhabit the seabed, ocean floor, or sediment substrate in marine and freshwater bodies, including sessile organisms (corals, sponges, barnacles) and mobile organisms (starfish, crabs, sea cucumbers).</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Benthic communities exist across all ocean depths, extending from shallow littoral zones down to the aphotic bathyal, abyssal plains, and deep hadal trenches (e.g., around deep-sea hydrothermal vents).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":49,"q":"With reference to 'Water Footprint', consider the following statements:\\n1. The Blue Water footprint refers to the volume of surface and groundwater consumed during production of a good.\\n2. The Green Water footprint refers to the volume of rainwater stored in the soil that evaporates or is incorporated by plants during growth.\\n3. The Grey Water footprint refers to the volume of freshwater required to assimilate the load of pollutants to maintain water quality standards.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> The Water Footprint Network classifies water consumption into three distinct metrics:</p><ul><li><b>Blue Water:</b> Surface and groundwater evaporated, incorporated, or transferred.</li><li><b>Green Water:</b> Rainwater stored in the root zone of soil used by crops.</li><li><b>Grey Water:</b> Volume of freshwater required to dilute wastewater to agreed ambient water quality standards.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":50,"q":"Which one of the following statements best describes the term 'Carbon Fertilization'?","options":["Increased plant growth due to higher concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere","Increased temperature of Earth due to higher concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere","Increased acidity of oceans as a result of increased concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere","Adaptation of all living beings on Earth to climate change brought about by the increased concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 1:</b> <b>Carbon Fertilization</b> (or the $CO_2$ fertilization effect) refers to the enhanced rate of photosynthetic carbon assimilation and biomass accumulation in terrestrial plants driven by elevated atmospheric carbon dioxide $(CO_2)$ concentrations, particularly in $C_3$ crop species.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":51,"q":"Consider the following:\\n1. Aarogya Setu\\n2. CoWIN\\n3. DigiLocker\\n4. DIKSHA\\n\\nWhich of the above are built on top of open-source digital platforms?","options":["1 and 2 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All four platforms are built on open-source software:</b> Under the Government of India's Policy on Adoption of Open Source Software, digital public goods including Aarogya Setu (code open-sourced on GitHub), CoWIN, DigiLocker, and DIKSHA are developed using open-source architectures.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":52,"q":"With reference to 'Web 3.0', consider the following statements:\\n1. Web 3.0 technology enables people to control their own data.\\n2. In Web 3.0 world, there can be blockchain-based social networks.\\n3. Web 3.0 is operated by users collectively rather than a corporation.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Web 3.0 represents a decentralized semantic web paradigm built on peer-to-peer blockchain protocols, enabling self-sovereign identity and user data ownership, decentralized autonomous organizations (DAOs), and censorship-resistant social media without centralized corporate monopolies.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":53,"q":"With reference to 'Software as a Service' (SaaS), consider the following statements:\\n1. SaaS buyers can customize the user interface and can often modify data fields.\\n2. SaaS users can access their data through their mobile devices.\\n3. Outlook, Hotmail and Yahoo! Mail are forms of SaaS.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> SaaS is a cloud computing software delivery model where applications are hosted centrally by a provider. Users access applications via browsers and mobile apps with customizable configurations, and consumer webmail platforms (such as Microsoft Outlook, Hotmail, Yahoo! Mail, and Gmail) are classic examples of SaaS.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":54,"q":"Which one of the following statements best reflects the idea behind the 'Fractional Orbital Bombardment System' (FOBS) often talked about in media?","options":["A hypersonic glide vehicle is launched into space to destroy incoming asteroids before hitting Earth.","A nuclear weapon is placed into low Earth orbit and de-orbited over the target area before completing a full circuit of Earth.","A spacecraft is landed on another planet after making several orbital maneuvers around Earth.","A missile is fired along a high suborbital parabolic trajectory that exits and re-enters the atmosphere directly."],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> A <b>Fractional Orbital Bombardment System (FOBS)</b> is a space-based strategic weapon delivery system where a warhead is launched into low Earth orbit and decelerated using retrograde thrusters to de-orbit towards its target before completing a full revolution, bypassing conventional early warning radar systems.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":55,"q":"Which one of the following is the context in which the term 'Qubit' is mentioned?","options":["Cloud Services","Quantum Computing","Visible Light Communication Technology","Wireless Communication Technology"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> A <b>Qubit</b> (quantum bit) is the fundamental unit of quantum information in <b>Quantum Computing</b>. Unlike classical binary bits (which exist strictly as 0 or 1), qubits leverage quantum superposition and entanglement to represent linear combinations of states simultaneously.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":56,"q":"Consider the following communication technologies:\\n1. Closed-circuit Television\\n2. Radio Frequency Identification\\n3. Wireless Local Area Network\\n\\nWhich of the above are considered Short-Range devices/technologies?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three technologies operate within short-range constraints:</b> Short-Range Devices (SRDs) cover localized wireless radio transmitters and hardwired/local network architectures including RFID tags, WLAN (Wi-Fi), and localized CCTV transmission infrastructure.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":57,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Biofilms':\\n1. Biofilms can form on medical implants within human tissues.\\n2. Biofilms can form on food and food processing surfaces.\\n3. Biofilms can exhibit antibiotic resistance.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Biofilms are syntrophic consortia of microorganisms encased in an extracellular polymeric substance (EPS) matrix. They readily form on medical implants (catheters, heart valves) and industrial food processing equipment, and provide high recalcitrance and resistance against antimicrobial and antibiotic treatments.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":58,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of 'Probiotics':\\n1. Probiotics are made of both bacteria and yeast.\\n2. The organisms in probiotics are found in foods we ingest, but they do not naturally occur in our gut.\\n3. Probiotics help in the digestion of milk sugars.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> Probiotics are live beneficial microorganisms encompassing lactic acid bacteria (e.g., <i>Lactobacillus</i>, <i>Bifidobacterium</i>) and yeasts (e.g., <i>Saccharomyces boulardii</i>). They produce lactase enzymes aiding the breakdown and digestion of milk sugars (lactose).</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> Beneficial probiotic bacterial strains naturally colonize the human gastrointestinal microbiome.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":59,"q":"In the context of vaccines manufactured to prevent COVID-19 pandemic, consider the following statements:\\n1. The Serum Institute of India produced COVID-19 vaccine named Covishield using mRNA platform.\\n2. Sputnik V vaccine is manufactured using vector based platform.\\n3. COVAXIN is an inactivated pathogen based vaccine.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are correct:</b> Sputnik V uses recombinant adenoviral vectors (Ad26 and Ad5). Covaxin (developed by Bharat Biotech) is a whole-virion inactivated SARS-CoV-2 vaccine.</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> Covishield (Oxford-AstraZeneca vaccine manufactured by Serum Institute) is a chimpanzee adenovirus viral vector vaccine (ChAdOx1), not an mRNA vaccine (mRNA vaccines are Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":60,"q":"If a major solar storm (solar flare) reaches the Earth, which of the following are the possible effects on the Earth?\\n1. GPS and navigation systems could fail.\\n2. Tsunamis could occur at equatorial regions.\\n3. Power grids could be damaged.\\n4. Intense auroras could occur over much of the Earth.\\n5. Forest fires could take place over much of the planet.\\n6. Orbits of the satellites could be disturbed.\\n7. Shortwave radio communication over sunlit regions could be disrupted.\\n\\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","options":["1, 2, 4, 5 and 6 only","2, 3, 5, 6 and 7 only","1, 3, 4, 6 and 7 only","1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1, 3, 4, 6 and 7 are correct effects of space weather:</b> Coronal mass ejections and solar storms ionize the upper atmosphere, causing geomagnetic storms that induce destructive currents in power grids, induce orbital drag on LEO satellites, disrupt high-frequency radio and satellite GPS signals, and trigger widespread auroral displays.</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 5 are incorrect:</b> Solar storms do not cause seismic crustal displacements (tsunamis require tectonic earthquakes) nor do they directly ignite terrestrial forest fires.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":61,"q":"Consider the following statements:\\n1. Climate Group is an international non-profit organization that drives climate action by building large networks and runs them.\\n2. The International Energy Agency in partnership with the Climate Group launched a global initiative 'EP100'.\\n3. EP100 brings together leading companies committed to driving energy productivity.\\n4. Some Indian companies are members of EP100.\\n5. The International Renewable Energy Agency (IRENA) is the secretariat to the 'Under2 Coalition'.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1, 2, 4 and 5","1, 3 and 4 only","2, 3 and 5 only","1, 2, 3, 4 and 5"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1, 3 and 4 are correct:</b> The Climate Group manages corporate climate initiatives including EP100 (which counts Indian companies like Mahindra & Mahindra and Dalmia Cement among its members).</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 5 are incorrect:</b> EP100 is led by The Climate Group in partnership with the Alliance to Save Energy (not IEA). The Climate Group serves as the secretariat of the Under2 Coalition (not IRENA).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":62,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Biochar' in agricultural practices:\\n1. Biochar can be used as a part of the growing medium in vertical farming.\\n2. When biochar is a part of the growing medium, it promotes the growth of nitrogen-fixing microorganisms.\\n3. When biochar is a part of the growing medium, it enables the growing medium to retain water for longer time.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Biochar is carbon-rich charcoal produced by biomass pyrolysis. Its high surface area and porous structure improve water retention in soils and soilless vertical farming substrates, and provide microhabitats that foster beneficial soil microbiota including symbiotic and free-living nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":63,"q":"With reference to the 'Deputy Speaker of Lok Sabha', consider the following statements:\\n1. As per the Rules of Procedure and Conduct of Business in Lok Sabha, the election of Deputy Speaker shall be held on such date as the Speaker may fix.\\n2. There is a mandatory provision that the election of a candidate as Deputy Speaker of Lok Sabha shall be from either the principal opposition party or the Ruling Party.\\n3. The Deputy Speaker has the same power as the Speaker when presiding over the sitting of the House and no appeal lies against his rulings.\\n4. The well-established parliamentary practice regarding the appointment of Deputy Speaker is that the motion is moved by the Speaker and duly seconded by the Prime Minister.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3","3 and 4 only","2 and 4 only"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> Under Rule 8 of the Lok Sabha Rules, the date of election of the Deputy Speaker is fixed by the Speaker. While presiding over the House, the Deputy Speaker exercises all powers of the Speaker, and their rulings cannot be challenged or appealed.</p><p><b>Statements 2 and 4 are incorrect:</b> The Constitution (Article 93) contains no mandatory provision requiring the Deputy Speaker to belong to the opposition party (it is a convention). Motions for election of the Deputy Speaker are moved and seconded by ordinary Members of Parliament, not by the Speaker and Prime Minister.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":64,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'G-20 Common Framework' for debt treatments:\\n1. It is an initiative endorsed by the G-20 together with the Paris Club.\\n2. It is an initiative to support Low-Income Countries with unsustainable debt.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The G-20 Common Framework for Debt Treatments beyond the DSSI was endorsed in November 2020 by the G-20 alongside the Paris Club to coordinate structural debt restructuring and relief for eligible low-income developing nations facing debt distress (e.g., Zambia, Ghana, Chad).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":65,"q":"With reference to the 'United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea' (UNCLOS), consider the following statements:\\n1. A coastal state has the right to establish the breadth of its territorial sea up to a limit not exceeding 12 nautical miles, measured from baselines determined in accordance with this Convention.\\n2. Ships of all States, whether coastal or land-locked, enjoy the right of innocent passage through the territorial sea.\\n3. The Exclusive Economic Zone shall not extend beyond 200 nautical miles from the baselines from which the breadth of the territorial sea is measured.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Under UNCLOS 1982, sovereign territorial waters extend up to 12 nautical miles, within which foreign flagged vessels enjoy the right of innocent passage (Article 17). The Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) extends up to 200 nautical miles from coastal baselines (Article 57).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":66,"q":"Which one of the following statements best reflects the issue with 'Senkaku Islands' sometimes mentioned in the news?","options":["It is generally believed that they are artificial islands made by a country around South China Sea.","China and Japan engage in maritime disputes over these islands in East China Sea.","A permanent military base has been set up there by USA to secure maritime navigation routes.","International Court of Justice declared them as no man's land and international heritage zone."],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> The <b>Senkaku Islands</b> (known as the Diaoyu Islands in China and Tiaoyutai in Taiwan) are an uninhabited island group in the <b>East China Sea</b> administered by Japan and contested by China.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":67,"q":"Consider the following pairs of countries and the regions frequently associated with conflicts:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Country</th><th>Region in news</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Chad</td><td>Anatolia</td></tr><tr><td>2. Mali</td><td>Sahel</td></tr><tr><td>3. Sudan</td><td>Darfur</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","All three pairs","None of the pairs"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Only two pairs (2 and 3) are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Mali:</b> Part of the semi-arid Sahel region facing extremist conflicts. (Correct)</li><li><b>Sudan:</b> Darfur is a western region of Sudan that experienced extensive ethnic and armed conflict. (Correct)</li><li>Pair 1 is incorrect: <b>Anatolia</b> (Asia Minor) constitutes the Asian landmass of <b>Turkey</b>, not Chad.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":68,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries' (OPEC):\\n1. It is a permanent intergovernmental organization created at the Baghdad Conference in 1960.\\n2. The headquarters of OPEC is located in Geneva, Switzerland.\\n3. OPEC+ is an alliance of OPEC members and non-OPEC oil producing countries including Russia.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> OPEC was founded in Baghdad in September 1960 by Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, and Venezuela. OPEC+ was formed in 2016 to coordinate crude oil production quotas with 10 non-OPEC producers including Russia, Kazakhstan, and Mexico.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> The headquarters of OPEC has been situated in <b>Vienna, Austria</b> since 1965 (having moved from Geneva).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":69,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Indian Patent System':\\n1. In India, plant varieties are not eligible for patent protection.\\n2. In India, there is no Intellectual Property Appellate Board (IPAB).\\n3. Compulsory licensing of a patented pharmaceutical drug can be issued under Section 84 of the Indian Patents Act, 1970.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Section 3(j) of the Patents Act, 1970 excludes plants, animals, and seeds from patentability (protected separately under the Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers' Rights Act, 2001). The Intellectual Property Appellate Board (IPAB) was formally abolished via the Tribunals Reforms Act, 2021 (transferring jurisdiction back to High Courts). Section 84 provides for compulsory licenses after three years of patent grant under specified public interest grounds.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":70,"q":"With reference to the 'United Nations General Assembly', consider the following statements:\\n1. The UN General Assembly can grant observer status to non-member States.\\n2. Inter-governmental organisations can seek observer status in the UN General Assembly.\\n3. Permanent Observers to the UN General Assembly can maintain missions at the UN headquarters.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> The UNGA grants Permanent Observer status to non-member sovereign states (e.g., the Holy See and the State of Palestine) as well as intergovernmental organizations (e.g., European Union, African Union, International Olympic Committee), enabling them to participate in debates and establish permanent missions at UN headquarters in New York.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":71,"q":"With reference to 'Indian Constitution', consider the following statements:\\n1. A candidate for election to the State Legislative Assembly must have completed at least 25 years of age.\\n2. A person shall be disqualified for being chosen as a member of the Legislative Council if he/she is less than 30 years of age.\\n3. The Governor of a State has the constitutional power to reserve a Bill passed by the State Legislature for the consideration of the President.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Under Article 173, the minimum age qualification is 25 years for the Legislative Assembly and 30 years for the Legislative Council. Under Article 200, the Governor is constitutionally empowered to reserve a Bill for the consideration of the President (governed by Article 201).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":72,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Tea Production and Trade in India':\\n1. India is the largest producer of tea in the world.\\n2. Assam and West Bengal together account for more than 75% of total tea production in India.\\n3. Black tea constitutes the largest share of tea produced in India.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 2 and 3 are correct:</b> Assam (approx. 50–52%) and West Bengal (approx. 28–30%) together produce over 80% of India's tea, and Black tea (CTC and Orthodox) comprises over 90% of domestic production.</p><p><b>Statement 1 is incorrect:</b> <b>China</b> is the world's largest producer of tea; India ranks second globally in production and is the largest consumer of black tea.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":73,"q":"Consider the following pairs of ancient Indian religious traditions and their iconic texts / canonical collections:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Religious Tradition</th><th>Canonical Text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Theravada Buddhism</td><td>Tripitaka (Pali Canon)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Digambara Jainism</td><td>Shatkhandagama</td></tr><tr><td>3. Saiva Siddhanta</td><td>Tirumurai</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Theravada Buddhism:</b> Canonical Pali <i>Tipitaka</i> (Vinaya, Sutta, Abhidhamma Pitakas).</li><li><b>Digambara Jainism:</b> Foundational canonical Prakrit text <i>Shatkhandagama</i> composed by Pushpadanta and Bhutabali.</li><li><b>Saiva Siddhanta:</b> The Twelve <i>Tirumurais</i> (including Tevaram, Tiruvacakam, and Periya Puranam) compiled by Nambiyandar Nambi.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":74,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Coal Controller's Organization' (CCO) in India:\\n1. CCO is the major source of Coal Statistics in Government of India.\\n2. It monitors progress of development of Captive Coal/Lignite blocks.\\n3. It hears and decides any objection to the Government's notification relating to acquisition of coal-bearing areas.\\n4. It ensures that coal is mined within prescribed safety standards set by the Director General of Mines Safety.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","3 and 4 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct:</b> The Coal Controller's Organization (subordinate office under Ministry of Coal) publishes the Coal Directory of India, inspects captive coal block development, and acts as the statutory competent authority to hear and settle objections regarding land acquisition under the Coal Bearing Areas (Acquisition and Development) Act, 1957.</p><p><b>Statement 4 is incorrect:</b> Mine safety regulations and enforcement fall under the jurisdiction of the Directorate General of Mines Safety (DGMS) under the Ministry of Labour and Employment, not the CCO.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":75,"q":"With reference to the 'Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana' (PMFBY), consider the following statements:\\n1. Farmers pay a uniform maximum premium of 2% for all Kharif food and oilseed crops and 1.5% for all Rabi crops.\\n2. The premium rate for commercial and horticultural crops is fixed at 5%.\\n3. The scheme covers post-harvest losses caused by unseasonal cyclonic rains up to a maximum period of two weeks from harvesting.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> PMFBY fixes actuarial premium rates payable by farmers at 2% for Kharif crops, 1.5% for Rabi crops, and 5% for annual commercial/horticultural crops (with the remaining premium subsidized equally by Central and State Governments). It also provides localized coverage for post-harvest losses up to 14 days for crops kept in 'cut and spread' condition in the field.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":76,"q":"With reference to the 'United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees' (UNHCR), consider the following statements:\\n1. It was created in 1950 by the United Nations General Assembly.\\n2. Its mandate is to lead and coordinate international action to protect refugees and resolve refugee problems worldwide.\\n3. It was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize twice (in 1954 and 1981).\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> UNHCR was established by the UN General Assembly in December 1950 with a mandate to protect refugees, forcibly displaced communities, and stateless people, and assist in their voluntary repatriation, local integration, or resettlement. UNHCR was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize twice, in 1954 and 1981.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":77,"q":"Consider the following pairs of rivers and the water bodies into which they empty:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>River</th><th>Drainage Body</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Mekong</td><td>Andaman Sea</td></tr><tr><td>2. Thames</td><td>Irish Sea</td></tr><tr><td>3. Volga</td><td>Caspian Sea</td></tr><tr><td>4. Zambezi</td><td>Indian Ocean</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","3 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4 only"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Pairs 3 and 4 are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Volga:</b> Europe's longest river, drains into the endorheic <b>Caspian Sea</b>. (Correct)</li><li><b>Zambezi:</b> Major southern African river flowing east into the <b>Indian Ocean</b> (Mozambique Channel). (Correct)</li><li>Pair 1 is incorrect: The <b>Mekong</b> drains into the <b>South China Sea</b>, not the Andaman Sea (Irrawaddy/Salween drain into the Andaman Sea).</li><li>Pair 2 is incorrect: The River <b>Thames</b> flows into the <b>North Sea</b>, not the Irish Sea.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":78,"q":"With reference to the 'G7' grouping, consider the following statements:\\n1. The G7 was originally formed in the 1970s in response to global economic problems including the first oil crisis.\\n2. Russia was a member of the group (making it G8) until its suspension in 2014 following the annexation of Crimea.\\n3. The G7 has a permanent treaty-based secretariat located in Brussels.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The Group of Seven (G7) originated in 1975 to coordinate responses to global economic and energy shocks. Russia was admitted in 1997 forming the G8, but was suspended indefinitely in 2014 after the annexation of Crimea.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The G7 is an informal bloc that does not have a formal charter, constitution, or permanent secretariat; its presidency rotates annually among member states.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":79,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Indian Sanitation Coalition' (ISC):\\n1. It is a multi-stakeholder platform launched under the aegis of the Federation of Indian Chambers of Commerce and Industry (FICCI).\\n2. Its vision is to enable safe and sustainable sanitation through multi-sectoral partnerships.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"correct":2,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Both statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The India Sanitation Coalition (ISC) was launched in 2015 at FICCI as a multi-stakeholder platform bringing together the private sector, government, NGOs, and academia to advocate, facilitate partnerships, and scale sustainable WASH (Water, Sanitation, and Hygiene) solutions across India.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 3.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":80,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Central Ground Water Authority' (CGWA):\\n1. It was constituted under Section 3(3) of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986.\\n2. It has the mandate to regulate and control groundwater development and management in the country.\\n3. India is the largest extractor of groundwater in the world, extracting more than 25% of the global total.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> CGWA was constituted under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986, following Supreme Court directions. It regulates indiscriminate groundwater extraction and issues NOCs for industrial and infrastructure projects. India is the world's largest extractor of groundwater (~250 billion cubic meters annually), accounting for over a quarter of total global groundwater extraction.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":81,"q":"With reference to the 'Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission' (ABDM), consider the following statements:\\n1. Private and public hospitals must adopt it.\\n2. As it aims to achieve universal health coverage, every citizen of India should be part of it ultimately.\\n3. It has seamless portability across the country.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement 3 is correct:</b> ABDM creates an open digital health ecosystem with national interoperability and seamless portability of electronic health records across healthcare providers nationwide.</p><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are incorrect:</b> Participation in ABDM (generating Ayushman Bharat Health Account / ABHA ID for citizens, and digital integration by private/public hospitals) is completely <b>voluntary</b>, not mandatory.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":82,"q":"With reference to 'National Health Authority' (NHA), consider the following statements:\\n1. It is the apex body responsible for implementing the Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (AB-PMJAY).\\n2. It functions as an attached office under the Ministry of Finance.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statement 1 is correct:</b> The National Health Authority (NHA) is the apex agency responsible for implementing AB-PMJAY and the Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> NHA functions as an attached office under the <b>Ministry of Health and Family Welfare</b>, not the Ministry of Finance.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":83,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee' (GEAC):\\n1. It is established under the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change (MoEFCC).\\n2. It is constituted under the Rules for the Manufacture, Use/Import/Export and Storage of Hazardous Microorganisms/Genetically Engineered Organisms or Cells, 1989 framed under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986.\\n3. It is responsible for appraisal of proposals relating to release of genetically engineered organisms and products into the environment including experimental field trials.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> GEAC is the apex statutory bio-safety regulatory body operating under the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change in accordance with the 1989 Rules under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986. It evaluates and grants environmental clearance for commercial release and field trials of GMOs and GM crops (e.g., Bt cotton, GM mustard).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":84,"q":"With reference to the 'United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification' (UNCCD), consider the following statements:\\n1. It is the sole legally binding international agreement linking environment and development to sustainable land management.\\n2. It addresses specifically the Arid, Semi-Arid and Dry Sub-Humid areas, known as the drylands.\\n3. The 14th Conference of the Parties (COP14) to UNCCD was hosted by India in New Delhi in 2019.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Adopted in 1994, UNCCD is the only legally binding global agreement focusing on dryland land degradation, desertification, and drought. India hosted UNCCD COP14 in New Delhi in September 2019, adopting the 'New Delhi Declaration' and committing to restore 26 million hectares of degraded land by 2030.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":85,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'World Trade Organization' (WTO) Agreement on Agriculture:\\n1. Green Box subsidies are considered non-distorting or minimally trade-distorting and are exempt from reduction commitments.\\n2. Blue Box subsidies include direct payments under production-limiting programmes.\\n3. Amber Box subsidies cover all domestic support measures considered to distort production and trade.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Under the WTO Agreement on Agriculture:</p><ul><li><b>Green Box:</b> Decoupled income support, R&D, and environmental subsidies with minimal/no trade-distorting effects (allowed without financial limits).</li><li><b>Blue Box:</b> Direct payments tied to supply/production-limiting quotas (exempt from reduction).</li><li><b>Amber Box:</b> Price-support mechanisms and input subsidies (subject to Aggregate Measurement of Support / AMS reduction limits).</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":86,"q":"With reference to the 'T-cell receptor' (TCR) and cellular immunity, consider the following statements:\\n1. T-cells are a type of white blood cell that mature in the thymus gland.\\n2. Cytotoxic T-cells directly identify and destroy infected host cells and tumor cells.\\n3. Helper T-cells produce antibodies directly and release them into the blood stream.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> T-lymphocytes originate in the bone marrow, mature in the thymus, and include Cytotoxic T-cells ($CD8^+$) that recognize antigen fragments on MHC Class I molecules and kill virus-infected and cancerous cells.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Antibodies are secreted exclusively by plasma cells differentiated from <b>B-lymphocytes</b>; Helper T-cells ($CD4^+$) release cytokines that stimulate B-cells, but do not secrete antibodies directly.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":87,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Nanoparticles' and their environmental interactions:\\n1. Some nanoparticles can cross biological membranes and enter cellular organelles.\\n2. Engineered nanoparticles of metal oxides can catalyze the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS).\\n3. Nanoparticles are completely biodegradable and cannot accumulate in aquatic food chains.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Due to nanoscale dimensions (1–100 nm), nanoparticles can penetrate cell membranes, cross blood-brain and placental barriers, and generate oxidative stress through reactive oxygen species (ROS) catalysis.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Many inorganic nanoparticles (such as titanium dioxide, silver, and carbon nanotubes) are non-biodegradable, persist in aquatic environments, and undergo trophic bioaccumulation.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":88,"q":"Match the following historical travellers with the royal dynasties in whose reigns they visited India:\\n\\n{{ <table class='table table-bordered text-center' style='width:100%; border: 1px solid #dee2e6;'><thead><tr style='background-color:#f8f9fa;'><th>Foreign Traveller</th><th>Visiting Era / Reign</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Megasthenes</td><td>Mauryan Empire (Chandragupta Maurya)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang)</td><td>Pushyabhuti Dynasty (Harshavardhana)</td></tr><tr><td>3. Abdur Razzaq</td><td>Vijayanagar Empire (Deva Raya II)</td></tr></tbody></table> }}\\n\\nWhich of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three pairs are correctly matched:</b></p><ul><li><b>Megasthenes:</b> Seleucid ambassador who visited the Mauryan court of Chandragupta Maurya at Pataliputra (author of <i>Indica</i>).</li><li><b>Hiuen Tsang:</b> Chinese pilgrim who studied at Nalanda and visited the court of King Harshavardhana (c. 630–645 CE).</li><li><b>Abdur Razzaq:</b> Persian envoy from the Timurid court of Shahrukh who visited Vijayanagar during the reign of Deva Raya II (Sangama dynasty) in 1443 CE.</li></ul><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":89,"q":"With reference to the 'Finance Commission', consider the following statements:\\n1. It is a quasi-judicial body constituted by the President under Article 280 of the Constitution.\\n2. The recommendations of the Finance Commission are laid before each House of Parliament along with an explanatory memorandum as to the action taken.\\n3. The Constitution of India explicitly specifies the educational and professional qualifications of the members of the Finance Commission.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The Finance Commission is a constitutional quasi-judicial body. Under Article 281, the President causes every recommendation made by the Finance Commission together with an explanatory memorandum to be laid before both Houses of Parliament.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The Constitution (Article 280(2)) authorized <b>Parliament</b> to determine by law the qualifications of members (which Parliament did via the Finance Commission Act, 1951), rather than defining them directly in the text of the Constitution.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":90,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana' (PMKVY):\\n1. It is the flagship scheme of the Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE).\\n2. It provides Short Term Training (STT) and Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL).\\n3. It is implemented through the National Skill Development Corporation (NSDC).\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> PMKVY is MSDE's flagship skill certification initiative implemented by the National Skill Development Corporation (NSDC). It provides industry-relevant Short Term Training (STT), Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) to certify informally acquired skills, and special skill development projects aligned with the National Skills Qualifications Framework (NSQF).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":91,"q":"With reference to the 'Sovereign Gold Bond' (SGB) Scheme, consider the following statements:\\n1. The SGBs are issued by the Reserve Bank of India on behalf of the Government of India.\\n2. The bonds bear interest at a fixed rate of 2.50% per annum on the nominal value.\\n3. The tenor of the bond is 8 years with an exit option from the 5th year.\\n4. Capital gains tax arising on redemption of SGB to an individual is completely exempt.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All four statements are correct:</b> Sovereign Gold Bonds are government securities denominated in grams of gold issued by the RBI. They pay a fixed annual coupon interest rate of 2.50% semi-annually, have a maturity period of 8 years (with encashment/redemption allowed after 5 years on interest payment dates), and capital gains tax on redemption at maturity is exempted for individuals under Section 47 of the Income-tax Act.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":92,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'FDI Policy' in India:\\n1. Non-Resident Indians (NRIs) investments on non-repatriable basis are deemed to be domestic investments at par with investments made by residents.\\n2. Foreign portfolio investment up to 10% in a listed company is treated as Foreign Direct Investment (FDI).\\n3. 100% FDI is permitted under the automatic route in the manufacturing sector.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 3 are correct:</b> Investments by NRIs on a non-repatriable basis through NRO accounts are treated as domestic investment outside FDI caps. 100% FDI is permitted under the automatic route for the manufacturing sector.</p><p><b>Statement 2 is incorrect:</b> Foreign portfolio investment of less than 10% of paid-up equity is classified as <b>Foreign Portfolio Investment (FPI)</b>; it is an investment of 10% or more that is classified and treated as FDI.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":93,"q":"With reference to the ancient history of India, consider the following statements regarding the 'Ashokan Inscriptions':\\n1. They were written mostly in Prakrit language and Brahmi script in the major parts of the subcontinent.\\n2. In the northwestern part of the empire, Ashokan inscriptions were written in Aramaic and Greek languages using Kharosthi script.\\n3. The name 'Ashoka' is explicitly mentioned in the Major Rock Edicts at Girnar and Kalsi.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Most Ashokan edicts are in Prakrit in the Brahmi script. In the northwest (e.g., Shahbazgarhi, Mansehra, Kandahar), inscriptions were engraved in Kharosthi, Aramaic, and Greek.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Most edicts refer to the emperor as <i>Devanampiya Piyadassi</i> ('Beloved of the Gods'); the personal name <b>Ashoka</b> appears explicitly only in four Minor Rock Edicts (Maski, Brahmagiri/Nittur, Udegolam in Karnataka, and Gujarra in MP), not at Girnar or Kalsi.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":94,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Monetary Policy Framework Agreement' in India:\\n1. It mandates the Reserve Bank of India to maintain consumer price index (CPI) inflation within the target band of 4% with a tolerance band of +/- 2%.\\n2. If the RBI fails to meet the inflation target for three consecutive quarters, it must submit a report to the Central Government explaining the failure.\\n3. The Monetary Policy Committee consists of eight members with the RBI Governor holding a veto power on all decisions.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> The flexible inflation targeting framework sets the target at 4% (+/- 2% tolerance, i.e., 2% to 6%). Failure occurs when average inflation is outside the band for three consecutive quarters, requiring a formal explanatory report to the Central Government under Section 45ZN of the RBI Act.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) consists of <b>6 members</b> (3 from RBI and 3 nominated by the Central Government). Decisions are made by majority vote; the RBI Governor has a casting vote in the event of a tie, but no absolute veto power.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":95,"q":"With reference to the physical geography of India, consider the following statements regarding 'Karewas':\\n1. They are thick lacustrine deposits of glacial clay and other sediments embedded with moraines.\\n2. They are found exclusively in the Kashmir Valley along the slopes of the Pir Panjal Range.\\n3. They are renowned for the cultivation of saffron (Zafran), almonds, and walnuts.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> Karewas are unconsolidated lacustrine (lake-bed) terrace deposits and glacial moraine formations unique to the Kashmir Valley (flanking the Jhelum and Pir Panjal). Their fine-grained loamy soils are ideal for cultivating world-famous Kashmir saffron (<i>Crocus sativus</i>), apples, and walnuts.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":96,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Indian Council of Agricultural Research' (ICAR):\\n1. It is an autonomous body under the Department of Agricultural Research and Education (DARE), Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare.\\n2. It was established in 1929 as the Imperial Council of Agricultural Research in pursuance of the Royal Commission on Agriculture.\\n3. The Union Minister of Agriculture is the ex-officio President of the ICAR Society.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":3,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>All three statements are correct:</b> ICAR is an autonomous apex agricultural research and education body founded in July 1929 following the report of the Royal Commission on Agriculture (Linlithgow Commission). It operates under DARE and is headed by the Union Minister of Agriculture as President of the ICAR Society.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 4.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":97,"q":"With reference to the modern constitutional history of India, the 'Rowlatt Act' (Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act, 1919) was passed by the Imperial Legislative Council to authorize which of the following measures?","options":["Imposition of strict pre-censorship on English newspapers across presidencies","Arrest and detention of any person suspected of terrorist activities without trial for up to two years","Mandatory confiscation of ancestral property of nationalist leaders","Complete dissolution of elected provincial municipal corporations"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Correct Option is 2:</b> The <b>Rowlatt Act of 1919</b> authorized the colonial government to imprison political suspects without warrant or judicial trial for up to two years (popularly summarized as <i>'No Dalil, No Vakil, No Appeal'</i>), which provoked nationwide Rowlatt Satyagraha led by Mahatma Gandhi and culminated in the Jallianwala Bagh massacre.</p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":98,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Black Carbon' (Soot):\\n1. It is a component of fine particulate matter $(PM_{2.5})$ formed by the incomplete combustion of fossil fuels, biofuels, and biomass.\\n2. It warms the Earth by absorbing solar radiation and decreasing the albedo of snow and ice when deposited in glacial regions.\\n3. Black carbon remains suspended in the atmosphere for centuries, similar to carbon dioxide.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":1,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Black carbon is a dark aerosol produced by incomplete combustion of biomass and fuels. It directly absorbs sunlight in the atmosphere and darkens snow/glacier surfaces (lowering surface albedo and accelerating glacial melt in the Himalayas and Arctic).</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Unlike $CO_2$ (which persists for centuries), black carbon is a <b>Short-Lived Climate Pollutant (SLCP)</b> that remains in the atmosphere for only a few days to weeks before being washed down by precipitation.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 2.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":99,"q":"With reference to ancient Indian trade guilds, consider the following statements regarding the 'Shrenis':\\n1. Shrenis served as banking institutions where people deposited money and earned interest.\\n2. Guilds established their own judicial rules (Shrenidharma) and regulations regarding quality control, prices, and wages.\\n3. Kings in ancient India had absolute discretionary power to dissolve guilds and confiscate their corporate assets at will without judicial sanction.","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> Ancient Indian craft and trade guilds (<i>Shrenis</i>) possessed autonomous internal administration, fixed commodity prices and artisan wages, maintained merchant courts enforcing guild custom (<i>Shreni-dharma</i>), and functioned as corporate banking and credit institutions accepting endowments and deposits.</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> Ancient legal treatises (Dharmashastras, Manu, and Yajnavalkya) explicitly mandated that kings must respect, protect, and enforce the customary laws and autonomy of the Shrenis rather than arbitrarily confiscating guild property.</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"},{"year":2022,"paper":"GS1","question":100,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the 'Preamble' to the Constitution of India:\\n1. The Preamble is a part of the Constitution.\\n2. The Preamble can be amended under Article 368 of the Constitution, subject to the basic structure doctrine.\\n3. The Preamble is a source of substantive power to the legislature and can be directly enforced in a court of law.\\n\\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct":0,"ex":"{{ <div className='space-y-2'><p><b>Statements 1 and 2 are correct:</b> In the landmark <i>Kesavananda Bharati case (1973)</i>, the Supreme Court ruled that the Preamble is an integral part of the Constitution and can be amended under Article 368 without altering the Basic Structure (as done by the 42nd Amendment Act in 1976).</p><p><b>Statement 3 is incorrect:</b> The Preamble is neither a direct source of power to the legislature nor a limitation upon legislative powers, and it is strictly <b>non-justiciable</b> (its provisions are not directly enforceable in courts of law).</p><p><b>Correct Option is 1.</b></p></div> }}"}]`);export{e as default};